ce is emphasized. So it doesn't necessarily mean all are equally important. Some might be more important than others. So D might not be correct.\n\nGoing back to Option A, it says performance is the most important, but others can't be ignored. The context does say that work performance is emphasized, which could imply it's the most important, but I'm not entirely sure. However, the other options don't seem to fit as well. Option B is too absolute, and C and D don't align with the emphasis on performance.\n\nWait, but the context says \"emphasis on the assessment of work performance,\" which might mean that performance is more important than the others, but not necessarily the only thing. So A seems to capture that idea. It's not saying performance is the only thing, just the most important, but others are still considered.\n\nSo, after considering all options, I think A is the correct inference.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A\n\nThe reasoning is that the context emphasizes work performance, suggesting it's the most important aspect, but other qualities are also considered. \n\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D.\n\nThe correct option is: A",
