his leaves H and I not together, which is good. But let's check the constraints:\n\n- F is not in the same sub-committee as G: Wait, in this case, F is in all three, and G is in two, including sub-committee 1 and 3. So, F and G are in sub-committees 1 and 3, but they are in the same sub-committees, which violates the constraint that F is not in the same sub-committee as G. So, this arrangement is invalid.\n\nSo, I need to adjust. Let's try:\n\nSub-committee 1: F, H, I\nSub-committee 2: F, M, P\nSub-committee 3: F, G, (someone)\n\nWait, but H and I can't be together. So, if F is in all three, then in each sub-committee, F is present, but the other two must not include both H and I.\n\nWait, perhaps:\n\nSub-committee 1: F, H, M\nSub-committee 2: F, I, P\nSub-committee 3: F, G, (needs to be someone not conflicting)\n\nWait, but G can't be with F in the same sub-committee as per the constraint? Wait, no, the constraint is that F is not in the same sub-committee as G. So, F and G cannot be together in any sub-committee. So, G must be in a sub-committee without F.\n\nWait, but F is in all three sub-committees, so G can't be in any of them. But that's impossible because G has to be in at least one sub-committee. So, this suggests that my initial assumption that F is the one in all three sub-committees might be incorrect, or perhaps I'm misapplying the constraints.\n\nWait, let me re-examine the constraints:\n\n1. One member is in all three sub-committees.\n2. F is not in the same sub-committee as G.\n3. H is not in the same sub-committee as I.\n\nSo, F and G cannot be together in any sub-committee. Similarly, H and I cannot be together in any sub-committee.\n\nSo, if F is in all three sub-committees, then G cannot be in any of them, which is impossible because G must be in at least one. Therefore, F cannot be the one in all three sub-committees. Similarly, G cannot be in all three because F can't be with G. So, the member who is in all three must be someone else, not F or 