ree are in one each. But wait, the problem states that one member is in all three sub-committees, which is three slots. So, that's one member contributing three slots, and the remaining two overlaps (since 9 - 6 = 3, but one member is already using three, so the remaining two overlaps must come from two members each in two sub-committees). So, in total, one member is in three sub-committees, two members are in two sub-committees each, and the remaining three members are in one sub-committee each.\n\nWait, that adds up: 3 (from the triple member) + 2*2 (from two double members) + 3*1 (from three single members) = 3 + 4 + 3 = 10, which is more than 9. Hmm, that doesn't add up. Maybe I'm miscalculating.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to think that the total number of member slots is 9, and we have six members. So, the number of times members are used more than once is 9 - 6 = 3. That means three members are in two sub-committees each, because each such member adds one extra slot. So, three members are in two sub-committees, contributing 3 extra slots, making the total 6 + 3 = 9. So, that makes sense: one member is in three sub-committees (contributing two extra slots), and two members are in two sub-committees each (contributing one extra slot each), totaling three extra slots. So, the distribution is: one member in three sub-committees, two members in two sub-committees each, and the remaining three members in one sub-committee each.\n\nNow, looking at the constraints:\n\n1. One member is in all three sub-committees. Let's call this person X. So, X is in sub-committees A, B, and C.\n\n2. F and G cannot be in the same sub-committee. So, F and G must be in different sub-committees.\n\n3. H and I cannot be in the same sub-committee. So, H and I must be in different sub-committees.\n\nNow, let's consider the options:\n\nOption A: One of F or G is a member of three sub-committees.\n\nBut from the constraints, we know that only one member is in all three sub-committees, which