blem states that one member is in all three, and the remaining two overlaps (since 9 - 6 = 3, but one member is in three, so the remaining two overlaps must come from two members each in two sub-committees). So, yes, there are two members in two sub-committees each. Therefore, Option D must be true because at least one member is in two sub-committees.\n\nWait, but the options are:\n\nA) One of F or G is a member of three sub-committees.\n\nB) One of H or I is a member of three sub-committees.\n\nC) P or M is a member of only one sub-committee.\n\nD) One member happens to be a member of the two sub-committees.\n\nSo, D must be true because we have two members in two sub-committees each. So, at least one of them is in two, making D correct.\n\nBut let me double-check. The question is asking which must be true. So, D is definitely true because we have two members in two sub-committees each. So, regardless of who they are, there must be at least one member in two sub-committees.\n\nWait, but the options are about specific members. Option D says \"One member happens to be a member of the two sub-committees.\" So, it's not about which specific member, just that at least one is in two. So, yes, D must be true.\n\nBut let me check the other options again to make sure.\n\nOption A: Could be true if X is F or G, but not necessarily. So, it's possible but not certain.\n\nOption B: Similarly, could be true if X is H or I, but not certain.\n\nOption C: P or M being in only one. Well, the three single members are each in one, so if P or M is among them, then yes. But it's possible that P or M is X or one of the two in two. So, it's possible that P is in one, making C true, but it's also possible that P is in two or three. So, C is not necessarily true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\n1. **Understanding the Problem:**\n   - 6 members: F, G, H, I, M, P.\n   - 3 sub-committees, each with 3 members.\n   - To