    {
      "index": 0,
      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to solve this logic problem about the switches S, T, W, X, Y, and Z. The question is, if both S and Z are turned on, which of the options A, B, C, or D must be true. Let me break down the conditions one by one and see how they apply when S and Z are on.\n\nFirst, let's list the given conditions:\n\n1. If W is turned on, then X must also be turned on. So, W \u2192 X. That means whenever W is on, X has to be on too. But if W is off, X can be either on or off.\n\n2. Only when S is turned off, then T can also be turned off. So, this is a bit tricky. It means that if S is on, T must be on. Because the only way T can be off is if S is off. So, in logical terms, S \u2192 T. Because if S is on, T can't be off. So, if S is on, T must be on.\n\n3. T and X cannot be turned on at the same time, nor can they be turned off at the same time. So, T and X are mutually exclusive in both their on and off states. That means if T is on, X must be off, and if T is off, X must be on. Similarly, if T is on, X can't be on, and if X is on, T can't be on. Same for their off states.\n\n4. If Y and Z are turned on at the same time, then W must also be turned on. So, Y \u2227 Z \u2192 W. That means whenever both Y and Z are on, W has to be on. But if either Y or Z is off, W can be either on or off.\n\nNow, the question states that both S and Z are turned on. So, S = on, Z = on.\n\nLet's see what this implies.\n\nFrom condition 2: Since S is on, T must be on. Because only when S is off can T be off. So, if S is on, T can't be off, so T must be on.\n\nFrom condition 3: Since T is on, X must be off. Because T and X can't be on at the same time. So, X = off.\n\nFrom condition 1: If W is on, then X must be on. But we just determined that X is off. So, if X is off, then W can't be on. Because if W were on, X would have to be on, which it isn't. Therefore, W must be off.\n\nFrom condition 4: Since Z is on, if Y is also on, then W must be on. But we just determined that W is off. So, if W is off, then Y can't be on at the same time as Z. Because if Y and Z were both on, W would have to be on, which it isn't. Therefore, Y must be off.\n\nSo, let's summarize what we have so far:\n\n- S = on\n- Z = on\n- T = on (because S is on)\n- X = off (because T is on)\n- W = off (because X is off)\n- Y = off (because if Y were on, W would have to be on, which it isn't)\n\nNow, let's look at the options:\n\nA) T is turned on and Y is turned off. Well, T is on and Y is off, so this seems to fit. But let's check the others.\n\nB) Both T and Y are turned off. But we have T on and Y off, so this isn't correct.\n\nC) Both W and T are turned on. But W is off and T is on, so this is wrong.\n\nD) X is turned on and Y is turned off. X is off, so this is incorrect.\n\nSo, the correct option is A.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A\n\nStep-by-Step Explanation:\n\n1. **Given S and Z are on.**\n2. **From condition 2 (S \u2192 T):** Since S is on, T must be on.\n3. **From condition 3 (T and X cannot be on at the same time):** Since T is on, X must be off.\n4. **From condition 1 (W \u2192 X):** Since X is off, W must be off.\n5. **From condition 4 (Y \u2227 Z \u2192 W):** Since Z is on and W is off, Y must be off.\n\nThus, T is on and Y is off, making option A correct.\n\nThe correct option is: A",
