l happening in the same places, but we're detecting more, maybe because of better instruments or methods. But this doesn't directly explain why the number of reported tornadoes has gone up while the actual number hasn't. It's more about where they're occurring, not the detection rate.\n\nOption D says the number of large and medium-sized tornadoes hasn't changed. If the size hasn't changed, but the number of reported ones has, that could mean that smaller tornadoes are now being detected, which were previously missed. So, the actual number of large and medium ones hasn't increased, but we're finding more small ones, leading to an overall increase in reported tornadoes. This would support the meteorologist's argument because it shows that the detection methods have improved, allowing us to find more tornadoes, even if the actual number hasn't gone up.\n\nWait, but the meteorologist's point is about the actual number not increasing, but the reported number has. So if the number of large and medium-sized tornadoes hasn't changed, but we're detecting more of the smaller ones, that would mean the total number of reported tornadoes has gone up, but the actual number (large and medium) hasn't. That makes sense because the actual number refers to the real count, not the detected ones. So D supports the argument.\n\nI think D is the best option because it directly relates to the idea that the detection of tornadoes has improved, allowing us to report more without the actual number increasing.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D\n\nThe meteorologist's argument hinges on the idea that the actual number of tornadoes hasn't increased, but the reported number has gone up due to better detection methods. Option D supports this by indicating that the number of large and medium-sized tornadoes hasn't changed, suggesting that improvements in detection have led to the reporting of more smaller tornadoes, thus increasing the reported count without an actual increase in the number of 