isk.\n\nWait, but I'm supposed to find which option questions the statement. The original statement says that eating less meat can protect against asthma. If AGEPs are not only from meat but also from other sources, does that affect the conclusion? Option B says AGEPs mainly come from meat but not exclusively. So if they come from other sources too, maybe the reduction in meat consumption isn't the only factor. But the original statement is about meat as a source, so B supports it rather than questioning it.\n\nWait, maybe I'm misunderstanding. The original statement says that eating less meat can protect children from asthma risk because AGEPs are released in meat. If AGEPs are also released from other foods, then maybe the reduction in meat isn't the only way to reduce AGEP exposure. But the question is about which option questions the statement, meaning it weakens the conclusion that less meat protects against asthma.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the question is about whether the association is direct or if other factors are involved. Option C says AGEPs can cause other things besides asthma, which might mean that the effect on asthma isn't solely due to AGEPs, but that's more about the mechanism than questioning the statement's conclusion.\n\nWait, perhaps I'm overcomplicating. Let me think again. The original statement is that AGEPs in meat cause asthma, so eating less meat reduces asthma risk. If AGEPs are not only from meat but also from other sources, does that mean that reducing meat isn't the only way to reduce AGEP exposure? But the original statement is about meat as a source, so B supports it.\n\nWait, maybe the question is about whether the association is direct. If AGEPs are also found in other foods, then the statement's conclusion that less meat is protective might not be as strong because other foods could also contribute. But I'm not sure if that's the case.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the question is about whether the association is causal. If AGEPs are