t-worth individuals, perhaps the claims would be lower because they're less likely to need medical services. But I'm not sure if this directly addresses the main issue of insufficient income to cover claims.\n\nOption C suggests insuring only those individuals who were rejected by other companies for similar policies. That's interesting. If other companies rejected them, it might mean that they are higher risk or have more severe conditions. Insuring only those could lead to higher claims, which would increase the company's losses. So this seems like the opposite of what they want. They would be taking on more risk, which isn't good for minimizing losses.\n\nOption D is about attracting middle-aged customers who are unlikely to submit claims for many years. Middle-aged people generally have fewer health issues and might not need medical services as often as elderly people. So if they target these customers, the claims might come in later, but the company can set premiums lower because the risk is lower. This could help in reducing the immediate risk of insufficient income, as the claims would come in over a longer period, and the premiums are lower to begin with. However, I'm not sure if this addresses the main issue of the policy being for elderly people. The policy is specifically for elderly people with elderly diseases, so maybe targeting middle-aged customers isn't the right approach.\n\nWait, but the policy is for elderly people, so maybe the company should focus on that demographic. So, perhaps the best strategy is to target a specific group within the elderly who are less likely to have severe claims. But looking at the options, none of them directly target a specific subgroup of the elderly except maybe Option A, which excludes those with pre-existing conditions. But the policy is for those who do have these diseases, so Option A might not be the best because it excludes the intended group.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the company can adjust their premiums based on the risk factors. But the options don't mention that. So, among the given options, Option D seems better because it targets people less likely to make claims, thus reducing the risk of high claims in the near term. Even though the policy is for elderly people, if they can attract middle-aged customers, the claims might come later, but the company can manage that by having a longer payout period or adjusting their premium structure.\n\nWait, but the policy is specifically for elderly people with certain diseases, so maybe the company should focus on that group. But the options don't have that. So, perhaps the best strategy is to set lower premiums to attract a broader group, but that might increase the risk. Alternatively, maybe they can adjust their underwriting to include only those with milder conditions, which would reduce the expected claims.\n\nBut looking back, Option A is insuring only those without serious diseases as children. That might reduce the risk because they don't have pre-existing conditions, so their claims might be lower. But the policy is for those who do have these diseases, so maybe this option is not ideal because it excludes the intended customers. However, if they exclude those with pre-existing conditions, they might not get enough customers to make the premium low enough.\n\nOption D is about middle-aged customers, which might not have the same health issues, so their claims would be lower. But the policy is for elderly people, so maybe this isn't the right target. However, the question is about minimizing losses, so perhaps the best approach is to reduce the risk of high claims, even if it means a narrower customer base.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the company can adjust their premium to be lower but still cover the costs, but the options don't mention that. So, among the options, Option D seems to be the best because it reduces the risk of high claims by targeting people less likely to need medical services, thus minimizing losses.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n1. The company wants to minimize losses, which means reducing the risk of insufficient income to cover claims.\n2. Option A excludes those with pre-existing conditions, potentially reducing claims but also limiting customer base.\n3. Option B targets wealthy individuals, which might not directly address claim risk but could affect premium rates.\n4. Option C increases risk by insuring rejected individuals, leading to higher claims.\n5. Option D targets middle-aged customers with lower claim likelihood, reducing immediate risk and potential losses.\n6. Even though the policy is for elderly people, targeting those less likely to claim helps minimize losses.\n\nAnswer: D",
