liquid water. If sodium salts can't form without liquid water, then their presence would mean water exists on Enceladus.\n\nWait, so both B and D seem relevant. Let me think again. The context says that sodium salts are found, leading to the inference of liquid water and an underground sea. So the reasoning is that sodium salts indicate water, which could be in the form of an underground sea.\n\nOption D is a straightforward cause-effect relationship: presence of sodium salts \u2192 presence of liquid water. That directly supports the conclusion. Option B is more about the form of water, suggesting that water can't be on the surface, so it must be underground. But the question is about whether the presence of sodium salts supports the conclusion, not necessarily the form of water.\n\nSo, the best support would be that sodium salts can only exist if there's liquid water, which is option D. It directly links the presence of sodium salts to the presence of liquid water, which is exactly what the conclusion is about.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D\n\nThe reasoning is that option D directly supports the conclusion by stating that sodium salts can only exist if there is liquid water, which aligns with the inference that Enceladus has liquid water and possibly an underground sea.\n\nThe correct option is: D",
