      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which option is supported by the given context about zebra mussels. Let me read the context again carefully. It says that zebra mussels are a nuisance because they clog pipes in nuclear and water plants along the Great Lakes, but they have some good qualities too. Specifically, they feed on algae by filtering water, which means that when they're released into the discharge streams of chemical plants, they help improve water quality by removing some hazardous wastes.\n\nNow, the question is asking which of the options A to D is most strongly supported by the context. I need to go through each option one by one and see if the context supports it.\n\nStarting with option A: It says there's no mechanical means to clear the intake pipes by scraping mussels off. The context doesn't mention anything about mechanical methods or whether such methods exist. It only talks about the mussels clogging pipes and their benefits. So, I don't think A is supported because the context doesn't provide information about mechanical solutions.\n\nMoving on to option B: It claims that zebra mussels arrived in the Great Lakes via transatlantic freighters and are rapidly displacing native clams because they have no natural enemies there. The context doesn't mention anything about how the mussels arrived in the Great Lakes or their interaction with native species like clams. It only states that they clog pipes and help with water quality. So, B isn't supported either.\n\nNext is option C: If the mussels spread to the Mississippi River where clams are used for a pearls industry, the industry would collapse because mussels are unsuitable. The context doesn't talk about mussels spreading to other regions or their impact on specific industries like pearl farming. It only discusses their effect on water quality. Therefore, C isn't supported.\n\nFinally, option D: It states that any hazardous waste removed by mussels from chemical plant discharge will remain hazardous if the mussels don't transform it, and thus they are still hazardous waste. The context mentions that mussels remove hazardous wastes from water streams, but it doesn't explicitly say whether the waste remains hazardous. However, logically, if the mussels don't process the waste, it would still be considered hazardous. So, D seems to be supported because it's a logical conclusion from the context.\n\nWait, but I should make sure. The context says the mussels remove some hazardous wastes, but it doesn't specify whether they transform them or not. So, if they don't transform, the waste remains, making it still hazardous. So, D is a valid conclusion based on the given information.\n\nSo, after evaluating all options, D is the one that's most strongly supported.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D\n\nThe reasoning is that the context states zebra mussels remove hazardous wastes from chemical plant discharge. If they don't transform the waste, it remains hazardous, making D supported.\n\nThe correct option is: D",
