her winning the prize causes longer life. It just provides a data point that some Nobel laureates didn't live long. So C might weaken the argument a bit, but not as much as B.\n\nOption D: Scholars who won the Nobel Prize in Physics have long lifespans, but none in Economics. Wait, this is a bit confusing. If physics laureates live longer, but none in Economics have won it, does that mean that Economics laureates don't live longer? But the expert's point is that Economics laureates do live longer. So if D is true, it actually supports the expert's claim because it shows that Physics laureates do live longer, but Economics laureates don't. But wait, the expert's data says Economics laureates do live longer. So D seems to contradict the expert's conclusion, but I'm not sure. Let me think again. The expert's conclusion is that winning the Nobel Prize in Economics leads to longer life. D says that Physics laureates live longer, but no Economics laureates have won it. So maybe the Nobel in Physics is a different award, and the expert's point is about Economics specifically. So D doesn't directly address the cause of longer life in Economics laureates. It just says that Physics laureates do live longer, but that doesn't necessarily mean that Economics laureates don't. So D might not be the best answer.\n\nWait, but the expert's conclusion is about Economics laureates. So if D says that Physics laureates live longer, but no Economics laureates have won it, does that mean that Economics laureates don't live longer? No, because the expert's data shows that Economics laureates do live longer. So D doesn't weaken the expert's conclusion; it actually supports it because it shows that Physics laureates do live longer, which is a different award. So D isn't the right answer.\n\nSo, going back, the best option to weaken the expert's conclusion is B because it suggests that the happiness from winning the prize is the real reason for longer life, not the prize itself. Alternatively,