    {
      "index": 0,
      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to solve this logical reasoning problem. Let me read through the context and the question again to make sure I understand everything.\n\nThe context says that nearly all factory employees who have completed their high school diploma have earned their factory safety certificate. They did this by easily completing an eight-week training course. Now, the high school graduates who haven't earned the certificate must be the ones with evening obligations that have prevented them from taking the course. Overall, most of the factory workers haven't earned the certificate.\n\nThe question is asking which of the given options must also be true based on the information provided. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nLet me break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, let's outline what we know:\n\n1. High school graduates who completed the training (which took 8 weeks) earned the certificate. They did this easily, so the process was straightforward for them.\n\n2. High school graduates who didn't earn the certificate had evening obligations that prevented them from taking the course. So, even though they had the diploma, they couldn't attend the training.\n\n3. Overall, most factory workers haven't earned the certificate. This means that the majority of the workforce doesn't have the certificate, but it doesn't specify their educational background.\n\nNow, let's look at each option:\n\nOption A: A large proportion of the factory workers have not graduated from high school.\nHmm, the context doesn't mention anything about workers not graduating. It only talks about those who have diplomas and those who haven't. So, we don't know if most workers have diplomas or not. This option is making a claim about a large proportion not having diplomas, but the context doesn't support that. So, I don't think A is necessarily true.\n\nOption B: A large proportion of students with high school diplomas have evening obligations that have prevented them from taking the eight-week training course.\nThis seems to align with the context. The context says that high school graduates who didn't earn the certificate had evening obligations. So, this is a direct restatement of the given information. Therefore, B must be true.\n\nOption C: Most of the workers who have earned the certificate are high school graduates.\nThe context says that nearly all high school graduates who completed the training earned the certificate. So, it's likely that most certificate holders are high school graduates. But wait, the context doesn't explicitly say that all high school graduates earned the certificate, only that those who did have evening obligations. So, maybe some high school graduates earned it, and others didn't. But the context doesn't specify how many high school graduates there are in total. It's possible that most certificate holders are high school graduates, but it's not certain. So, I'm not sure if C must be true.\n\nOption D: No factory worker without a high school diploma has completed the course and earned the certificate.\nThis is saying that anyone without a diploma hasn't earned the certificate. But the context doesn't say anything about people without diplomas. It only talks about high school graduates. So, we don't know if non-graduates have the certificate or not. The context doesn't provide information about them, so D can't be concluded as true.\n\nWait, let me think again about option C. The context says that nearly all high school diploma holders earned the certificate. So, if most high school graduates took the course and earned it, then most certificate holders are high school graduates. But what if some non-graduates also earned it? The context doesn't say, but it's possible. However, the context doesn't mention non-graduates at all, so maybe the only ones who earned the certificate are high school graduates. But the context doesn't explicitly state that. So, I'm not sure if C is necessarily true.\n\nBut looking back, the context says that high school graduates who didn't earn the certificate had evening obligations. So, high school graduates either earned the certificate or had evening obligations. It doesn't say anything about non-graduates. So, maybe non-graduates could have earned the certificate, but the context doesn't provide information about them. Therefore, C might not be necessarily true because it's possible that some non-graduates earned the certificate, but the context doesn't confirm that.\n\nSo, going back, option B is definitely true because it's a direct restatement of the context. Option A is not necessarily true because we don't know the proportion of non-graduates. Option C is uncertain because it's possible that some non-graduates earned the certificate, but the context doesn't confirm it. Option D is also not necessarily true because the context doesn't say anything about non-graduates.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be B.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\n1. The context states that high school graduates who completed the training earned the certificate.\n2. Those who didn't earn it had evening obligations.\n3. Option B directly reflects this information, stating that a large proportion of high school graduates couldn't attend the course.\n4. Options A, C, and D are not supported by the given information.\n\nAnswer: B",
