Now, the question states that only one of C1 or C2 is true. So, either C1 is true and C2 is false, or C2 is true and C1 is false.\n\nLet me analyze both scenarios.\n\nFirst, if C1 is true (P \u2228 E) and C2 is false (\u00ac(P \u2228 D)), which would mean that neither P nor D is true. So, if C2 is false, then both P and D are false. But if P is false, then for C1 to be true, E must be true. So, in this case, E is true, and P and D are false.\n\nAlternatively, if C2 is true (P \u2228 D) and C1 is false (\u00ac(P \u2228 E)), which would mean that neither P nor E is true. So, if C1 is false, then both P and E are false. But if P is false, then for C2 to be true, D must be true. So, in this case, D is true, and P and E are false.\n\nSo, from these two scenarios, we have two possibilities:\n\n1. E is true, P and D are false.\n2. D is true, P and E are false.\n\nNow, looking at the options:\n\nOption B says P is false and D is true. That matches the second scenario.\n\nOption C says P is true and E is false. Wait, but in both scenarios, P is false. So, Option C can't be correct because in both cases, P is false.\n\nOption A says both P and D are true. But in both scenarios, either E or D is true, but not both. Because if E is true, D is false, and vice versa. So, A is not possible.\n\nOption D says neither P nor D is true. But in both scenarios, either E or D is true, so D can't be correct.\n\nWait, but let me think again. The question says that only one of the two conclusions is true. So, either C1 is true and C2 is false, or C2 is true and C1 is false.\n\nIn the first case, C1 is true (so either P or E), and C2 is false (so neither P nor D). So, if C2 is false, then P is false and D is false. Therefore, for C1 to be true, E must be true. So, E is true, P and D are false.\n\nIn the second case, C2 is true (so either P or D), and C1 is false (so neither P nor E). So, if C1 is false, then P is false and E is false. Therefore, for C2 to be true, D must be true. So, D is true, P and E are false.\n\nSo, in both cases, P is false. Now, looking at the options:\n\nOption B: P is false, D is true. That's one scenario.\n\nOption C: P is true, E is false. But in both scenarios, P is false, so C is not possible.\n\nOption A: P and D both true. But in both scenarios, either E or D is true, but not both. So, A is not possible.\n\nOption D: Neither P nor D. But in both scenarios, either E or D is true, so D is not possible.\n\nWait, but the question is asking which conclusion can be inferred if only one of the two assertions is true. So, the correct answer must be either B or C, but from the analysis, only B is possible because in the first scenario, E is true, but the options don't include E. Wait, no, the options are about P and D.\n\nWait, perhaps I'm overcomplicating. Let me think differently.\n\nThe two conclusions are:\n\nC1: P \u2228 E\n\nC2: P \u2228 D\n\nOnly one of C1 or C2 is true.\n\nSo, if C1 is true, then either P or E is true. But if C2 is false, then neither P nor D is true. So, if C2 is false, then P is false and D is false. Therefore, for C1 to be true, E must be true. So, in this case, E is true, P and D are false.\n\nAlternatively, if C2 is true, then either P or D is true. But if C1 is false, then neither P nor E is true. So, if C1 is false, then P is false and E is false. Therefore, for C2 to be true, D must be true. So, D is true, P and E are false.\n\nSo, in both cases, P is false. Now, looking at the options:\n\nOption B: P is false, D is true. That's one scenario.\n\nOption C: P is true, E is false. But in both scenarios, P is false, so C is not possible.\n\nOption A: P and D both true. But in both scenarios, either E or D is true, but not both. So, A is not possible.\n\nOption D: Neither P nor D. But in both scenarios, either E or D is true, so D is not possible.\n\nWait, but the options don't include E. So, perhaps the correct answer is B because in one scenario, D is true and P is false, which matches option B.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I'm missing something. Let me check again.\n\nIf only one of C1 or C2 is true, then either:\n\n1. C1 is true and C2 is false: So, P \u2228 E is true, and P \u2228 D is false. For P \u2228 D to be false, both P and D must be false. Therefore, E must be true.\n\nSo, in this case, E is true, P and D are false.\n\n2. C2 is true and C1 is false: So, P \u2228 D is true, and P \u2228 E is false. For P \u2228 E to be false, both P and E must be false. Therefore, D must be true.\n\nSo, in this case, D is true, P and E are false.\n\nSo, in both cases, P is false. Now, looking at the options:\n\nOption B: P is false, D is true. That's the second scenario.\n\nOption C: P is true, E is false. But in both scenarios, P is false, so C is not possible.\n\nOption A: P and D both true. But in both scenarios, either E or D is true, but not both. So, A is not possible.\n\nOption D: Neither P nor D. But in both scenarios, either E or D is true, so D is not possible.\n\nWait, but the question is asking which conclusion can be inferred. So, the correct answer must be B because in one scenario, D is true and P is false, which matches option B.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the answer is C because in the other scenario, E is true, but the options don't include E. Wait, no, the options are about P and D.\n\nWait, maybe I'm overcomplicating. Let me think of it as a logical problem.\n\nWe have two statements:\n\nC1: P \u2228 E\n\nC2: P \u2228 D\n\nOnly one of C1 or C2 is true.\n\nSo, either C1 is true and C2 is false, or C2 is true and C1 is false.\n\nCase 1: C1 is true, C2 is false.\n\nC1: P \u2228 E = true\n\nC2: P \u2228 D = false\n\nFor C2 to be false, both P and D must be false.\n\nSo, P = false, D = false.\n\nThen, for C1 to be true, since P is false, E must be true.\n\nSo, E = true.\n\nSo, in this case, E is true, P and D are false.\n\nCase 2: C2 is true, C1 is false.\n\nC2: P \u2228 D = true\n\nC1: P \u2228 E = false\n\nFor C1 to be false, both P and E must be false.\n\nSo, P = false, E = false.\n\nThen, for C2 to be true, since P is false, D must be true.\n\nSo, D = true.\n\nSo, in this case, D is true, P and E are false.\n\nNow, looking at the options:\n\nOption B: P is false, D is true. That's exactly the second case.\n\nOption C: P is true, E is false. But in both cases, P is false, so C is not possible.\n\nOption A: P and D both true. But in both cases, either E or D is true, but not both. So, A is not possible.\n\nOption D: Neither P nor D. But in both cases, either E or D is true, so D is not possible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\n1. The two conclusions are C1 (P \u2228 E) and C2 (P \u2228 D), where P is Jupiter's pushing into smaller orbits, E is expelling, and D is driving out of the solar system.\n\n2. Only one of C1 or C2 is true.\n\n3. Analyzing both scenarios:\n   - If C1 is true (P \u2228 E) and C2 is false (\u00ac(P \u2228 D)), then P and D must be false, making E true.\n   - If C2 is true (P \u2228 D) and C1 is false (\u00ac(P \u2228 E)), then P and E must be false, making D true.\n\n4. In both cases, P is false. The only option matching this is B, where D is true and P is false.\n\nAnswer: B",
