n anything about underweight children. It only talks about obesity increasing. So there's no information to support or refute this option. It's probably not correct.\n\nSo, considering all this, Option B seems like the most logical conclusion because an increase in obesity could be due to less physical exercise. Option A isn't necessarily true because we don't know the total number of children. Option C is incorrect because not every obese child is above average. Option D is unrelated to the given information.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be B.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n1. The context states that obesity is defined by a weight-to-height ratio exceeding 80% of peers, and obesity rates have increased in Linjiang over 15 years.\n2. Option A is incorrect because the context doesn't provide data on the total number of children or non-obese children, so we can't conclude their trend.\n3. Option B is plausible as increased obesity could indicate reduced physical exercise, aligning with the context.\n4. Option C is incorrect because obesity doesn't mean every child is above average weight.\n5. Option D is unrelated as there's no data on underweight children.\n\nAnswer: B",
