      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out what argumentative strategy Judith is using in her reply to Anthony. Let me break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, Anthony is saying that because over 80% of heroin users have a marijuana history, it proves that marijuana leads to heroin use. That sounds like a correlation being mistaken for causation. Just because two things are correlated doesn't mean one causes the other.\n\nJudith counters this by saying that Anthony's statistics don't prove it. She points out that 100% of heroin users had a history of drinking water. Hmm, that's an interesting point. She's using an extreme example to show that Anthony's reasoning is flawed.\n\nSo, what's Judith doing here? She's taking Anthony's general argument and showing that it leads to a clearly false conclusion. Anthony's logic suggests that if you use marijuana, you'll get into heroin use. But if everyone who uses heroin had drunk water, that's a different story. It's an extreme example to show that Anthony's reasoning is not valid because it doesn't hold up when you push it to the extreme.\n\nLooking at the options, option D says Judith is demonstrating that Anthony's reasoning is flawed by showing it leads to false conclusions. That fits because she's using an example that makes Anthony's argument absurd. So, the answer should be D.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D",
