  "choices": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this question based on the given context. Let me start by understanding the context first. The context says that native speakers perceive sentences as sequences of separate words, but this is actually an illusion. It explains that travelers who don't know a local language hear an uninterrupted stream of sounds instead of distinct words. \n\nSo, the main point here is that our perception of language as broken into words is not real\u2014it's just how our brains process it. Now, the question is asking which assumption the argument depends on. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nLet me break down each option one by one.\n\nOption A says that native speakers' perceptions aren't more accurate than those of non-speakers. Hmm, the context doesn't really talk about accuracy. It just states that the perception is an illusion. So I'm not sure if A is the right assumption. It might be, but I'm not certain yet.\n\nOption B claims that accomplished non-native speakers don't perceive sentences as streams. But the context doesn't mention anything about accomplished speakers. It's about travelers who don't know the language, not necessarily about skilled non-native speakers. So B might not be the right answer.\n\nOption C states that it's impossible to understand sentences if they're an uninterrupted stream. The context does suggest that without the word breaks, it's unintelligible, so this seems to align with the argument. The argument is saying that because we perceive words as separate, we can understand, but in reality, it's a stream. So C seems relevant.\n\nOption D suggests that people don't pay attention to their own language's sounds as much as to unfamiliar ones. The context doesn't talk about attention levels, just perception as an illusion. So D might not be the assumption here.\n\nPutting it all together, the argument hinges on the idea that the perception of words as separate is an illusion, leading to the conclusion that without the breaks, it's unintelligible. So the assumption is that if it's a stream, you can't understand it. That points to option C.\n</think>\n\nThe argument depends on the assumption that an uninterrupted stream of sounds makes sentences unintelligible, which is option C.\n\nThe correct option is: C",
      "logprobs": null,
      "finish_reason": "stop",
      "stop_reason": null,
      "prompt_logprobs": null
    }
  ],
