n't allow for multiple answers.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in assuming both scenarios are possible. Let me check again.\n\nFrom Equation (1): Zhao + Qian = Sun + Li.\n\nIf Zhao is the heaviest, then Zhao > Li, which would mean Qian < Sun.\n\nBut from Equation (4), Zhao > Sun, so if Zhao > Li, then Qian must be less than Sun.\n\nBut from Equation (3), Li > Qian, so Qian is less than Li.\n\nSo, the order would be Zhao > Li > Qian < Sun.\n\nWait, but from Equation (4), Zhao > Sun, so if Zhao > Li, then Li must be less than Zhao, but greater than Qian.\n\nSo, the order would be Zhao > Li > Qian < Sun.\n\nWait, but that would mean Sun is greater than Qian, which is possible.\n\nBut from Equation (1), Zhao + Qian = Sun + Li.\n\nIf Zhao > Li, then Qian must be less than Sun to balance it.\n\nSo, Sun > Qian.\n\nBut from Equation (4), Zhao > Sun, so if Zhao > Sun and Sun > Qian, then Zhao > Sun > Qian.\n\nBut from Equation (3), Li > Qian.\n\nSo, the order could be Zhao > Li > Qian < Sun.\n\nWait, but that would mean Sun is greater than Qian, but less than Zhao.\n\nBut from Equation (1), Zhao + Qian = Sun + Li.\n\nIf Zhao > Li, then Qian must be less than Sun.\n\nSo, Sun > Qian.\n\nBut from Equation (4), Zhao > Sun, so if Zhao > Sun and Sun > Qian, then Zhao > Sun > Qian.\n\nBut from Equation (3), Li > Qian.\n\nSo, the order could be Zhao > Li > Qian < Sun.\n\nBut that would mean Sun is greater than Qian but less than Zhao.\n\nBut from Equation (1), Zhao + Qian = Sun + Li.\n\nIf Zhao > Li, then Qian must be less than Sun.\n\nSo, Sun > Qian.\n\nBut from Equation (4), Zhao > Sun, so if Zhao > Sun and Sun > Qian, then Zhao > Sun > Qian.\n\nBut from Equation (3), Li > Qian.\n\nSo, the order could be Zhao > Li > Qian < Sun.\n\nBut that would mean Sun is greater than Qian but less than Zhao.\n\nBut from Equation (1), Zhao + Qian = Sun + Li.\n\nIf Zhao > Li, then Qian must be less than Sun.\n\nSo, Sun > Qian.\n\nBut from Equation (4), Zhao > Sun, so if Zhao > Sun and Sun > Qian, then Zhao > Sun > Qian.\n\nBut from Equation (3), Li > Qian.\n\nSo, the order could be Zhao > Li > Qian < Sun.\n\nBut that would mean Sun is greater than Qian but less than Zhao.\n\nBut from Equation (1), Zhao + Qian = Sun + Li.\n\nIf Zhao > Li, then Qian must be less than Sun.\n\nSo, Sun > Qian.\n\nBut from Equation (4), Zhao > Sun, so if Zhao > Sun and Sun > Qian, then Zhao > Sun > Qian.\n\nBut from Equation (3), Li > Qian.\n\nSo, the order could be Zhao > Li > Qian < Sun.\n\nBut that would mean Sun is greater than Qian but less than Zhao.\n\nBut from Equation (1), Zhao + Qian = Sun + Li.\n\nIf Zhao > Li, then Qian must be less than Sun.\n\nSo, Sun > Qian.\n\nBut from Equation (4), Zhao > Sun, so if Zhao > Sun and Sun > Qian, then Zhao > Sun > Qian.\n\nBut from Equation (3), Li > Qian.\n\nSo, the order could be Zhao > Li > Qian < Sun.\n\nBut that would mean Sun is greater than Qian but less than Zhao.\n\nBut from Equation (1), Zhao + Qian = Sun + Li.\n\nIf Zhao > Li, then Qian must be less than Sun.\n\nSo, Sun > Qian.\n\nBut from Equation (4), Zhao > Sun, so if Zhao > Sun and Sun > Qian, then Zhao > Sun > Qian.\n\nBut from Equation (3), Li > Qian.\n\nSo, the order could be Zhao",
