      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read it again to make sure I understand it correctly.\n\nThe context says that an advertising firm has campaigns for both TV and the Internet, and these campaigns are similar in size. Now, the TV audience is one thousand times bigger than the Internet audience. But here's the kicker: the firm makes two-thirds of its revenue from Internet ads. That seems a bit confusing because if TV has a much larger audience, you'd think it would bring in more revenue, right? So there's a paradox here that needs to be resolved.\n\nThe question is asking which statement, if true, would explain this paradox. The options are A, B, C, D. Let me go through each one.\n\nOption A says the internet and TV ads are identical. Hmm, that doesn't make much sense. If they were identical, why would the firm make more money from one medium? Maybe they charge more for Internet ads, but the context doesn't mention pricing differences. So I'm not sure if this resolves the paradox.\n\nOption B claims the firm has more experience with TV ads. More experience might mean they're more efficient or charge more, but again, the context doesn't talk about pricing or efficiency. It just mentions revenue from each medium. So I'm not convinced this is the right answer either.\n\nOption C states the firm pays more for Internet ads. Wait, if they pay more for Internet ads but make more revenue from them, that doesn't add up. If they pay more, how do they make more money? Unless the effectiveness is different. But the question is about resolving the paradox of revenue despite a larger audience on TV. So this might not be the right path.\n\nOption D says Internet ads allow the firm to target audiences more accurately. That makes sense. If the firm can target a specific audience better with Internet ads, even though the audience size is smaller, each ad might be more valuable. For example, if they can reach high-value customers who spend more, or if the ads are more relevant, each ad might generate more revenue. So even though there are fewer people, each ad could bring in more money, leading to higher overall revenue from Internet ads.\n\nWait, let me think again. The TV audience is one thousand times larger, but the firm gets two-thirds of its revenue from Internet ads. So maybe each Internet ad is more profitable. If the ads on TV are less effective or less targeted, they might not generate as much revenue per ad. So having better targeting on the Internet could mean each ad is more valuable, thus making up for the smaller audience size.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the correct answer should be D because it explains why Internet ads, despite the smaller audience, contribute more to the firm's revenue.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\nThe paradox arises because TV has a much larger audience, yet the firm earns more from Internet ads. To resolve this, we consider that better targeting on the Internet (Option D) could mean each ad is more effective or targets high-value customers, making each ad more profitable. This explains how Internet ads can generate more revenue despite a smaller audience.\n\nThe correct option is: D",
