ement-oriented. Maybe the answer is none of them, but that can't be. Alternatively, perhaps the answer is C because it's about the employees' work, not Xiao Zhang's own achievements. But the question is about which is NOT showing achievement motivation, so maybe C is the correct answer because it's about the employees' work, not the leader's own achievement. Alternatively, maybe all are showing it except one. I'm a bit confused now. Let me think again.\n\nWait, the definition is about the individual's own pursuit. So if Xiao Zhang is motivating others to do their work well, that's about the team's success, not his own. But he's the leader, so he's probably motivated to lead well, which is still achievement-oriented. So maybe all options are showing achievement motivation except one. Alternatively, maybe the answer is C because it's about the employees, not the leader's own achievement. But I'm not sure. I think I need to pick the one that's not about personal achievement. So maybe C is the answer because it's about others' work, not the individual's own achievement.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C",
