      "index": 0,
      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read it again carefully.\n\nThe context is about a researcher who went back to a site where he had tagged armadillos the previous spring. He found most of them within a few hundred yards of where they were tagged last spring. He concluded that armadillos don't move rapidly into new territories.\n\nThe question is asking which assumption is required by the researcher's argument. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nFirst, I need to understand the researcher's argument. He observed that most recaptured armadillos were close to the tagging site. He concluded that they don't move quickly into new territories. So, the reasoning is that if they moved quickly, they wouldn't be found in the same area. Therefore, they don't move rapidly.\n\nNow, I need to find the assumption here. An assumption is something that must be true for the argument to hold. It's an unstated premise that connects the evidence to the conclusion.\n\nLet me look at each option.\n\nOption A: A large majority of the recaptured armadillos did not move to a new territory in the intervening summer and then move back to the old territory by the fall.\n\nHmm, so the researcher found them in the same area, but this option suggests that some moved away and then came back. Does this affect the conclusion? The researcher concluded that they don't move rapidly into new territories. If some moved away and came back, that might mean they do move, but maybe not quickly. However, the researcher's conclusion is that they don't move rapidly, not that they don't move at all. So, this might not be the required assumption because the conclusion is about not moving rapidly, not about not moving at all. So maybe A isn't the right answer.\n\nOption B: Predators did not kill any of the armadillos that had been tagged the previous spring.\n\nThis is about whether the tagged armadillos were killed by predators. If predators had killed some, then the recapture rate might be lower. But the researcher's argument is about movement into new territories, not about survival. So, even if some were killed, the fact that most were found nearby would still support the conclusion that they don't move rapidly. So, B might not be necessary because the argument doesn't hinge on whether the tagged armadillos survived. The researcher's focus is on their movement, not their survival. So B might not be the assumption.\n\nOption C: The tags identifying the armadillos cannot be removed by the armadillos, either by accident or deliberately.\n\nThis is about whether the armadillos can remove their own tags. If they could, then the researcher might not have accurately identified them. For example, if an armadillo moved away and removed its tag, the researcher might not recapture it. But the researcher found them in the same area, so if the tags were removable, that could affect the recapture rate. However, the researcher's argument is about movement into new territories, not about the tags being removed. The argument assumes that the tags are intact to identify the recaptured armadillos. So, if the tags could be removed, it might affect the data, but the researcher's conclusion is about movement, not about tag removal. Therefore, C might be an assumption because if the tags could be removed, the researcher's method would be flawed, but the argument's conclusion is about movement, not about tag integrity. So, maybe C is necessary because the researcher needs the tags to be intact to make the conclusion valid.\n\nOption D: Of the armadillos living in the area of the tagging site last spring, few were able to avoid being tagged by the researcher.\n\nThis is about whether most armadillos were tagged. If few avoided being tagged, then the researcher's tagging was effective. But the researcher's argument is about the movement of the recaptured armadillos. The fact that most were found nearby doesn't necessarily depend on whether most were tagged. The researcher's conclusion is about movement, not about the number of tagged individuals. So, D might not be necessary because the argument doesn't hinge on the number of tagged armadillos but on their movement.\n\nWait, but let me think again. The researcher's argument is that most recaptured were within a few hundred yards, so he concluded they don't move rapidly. The assumption here is that the tags are reliable, meaning the armadillos can't remove them. If they could, then the researcher might not have accurate recapture data. So, the assumption is that the tags are intact, which is option C.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the assumption is that the armadillos were not killed, but I think that's not necessary because the argument is about movement, not survival. So, the key assumption is that the tags are intact, so the researcher can accurately identify the recaptured armadillos. Therefore, the correct answer is C.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C\n\nThe researcher's argument relies on the assumption that the tags cannot be removed by the armadillos, ensuring accurate recapture identification. Without this, the data might be flawed, affecting the conclusion about their movement.\n\nThe correct option is: C",
