      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read it again carefully.\n\nThe context says that adults who work outside the home spend, on average, 100 minutes less each week preparing dinner than those who don't work outside the home. But then, when it comes to the dinners they eat at home, there's no significant difference in terms of nutritional value, variety, or number of courses. So, the question is asking which option resolves the discrepancy between these two pieces of information.\n\nHmm, so the first part is about time spent preparing dinner, and the second part is about the quality of the dinners eaten at home. At first glance, it seems contradictory because if they're spending less time preparing, you might expect their home dinners to be less nutritious or less varied, but that's not the case here.\n\nLet me break down the options one by one to see which one makes the most sense.\n\nOption A: The fat content of dinners at home for non-working adults is 25% higher than national guidelines. Wait, but the context doesn't mention anything about fat content. It only talks about time spent and the quality of dinners. So this might not directly address the discrepancy because it's only talking about one aspect of the dinner\u2014fat content. Plus, the problem is about the overall quality, not just fat.\n\nOption B: Working adults eat dinner at home 20% less often than non-working adults. Oh, this is interesting. If they're eating out more, they might not be preparing as much, which could explain why they spend less time preparing dinners. But wait, the context says that non-working adults spend more time preparing, not less. So if working adults eat out more, they might have less time preparing, but the problem is that non-working adults are spending more time preparing but their dinners are just as good. So maybe this could explain why they have less time preparing but similar dinner quality because they're eating out more. But I'm not sure if this directly resolves the discrepancy because the time spent is about preparation, not about eating out.\n\nOption C: Non-working adults prepare breakfast more often. This is about a different meal, breakfast, not dinner. The context is about dinner preparation time and dinner quality. So this might not help with the discrepancy because it's a different meal and doesn't explain why dinner time is less for working adults.\n\nOption D: Working adults spend 2 hours less per day on all household responsibilities, including dinner preparation. So if they're spending less time on all household tasks, including dinner, that could explain why they spend less time preparing dinner. But wait, the context says they spend 100 minutes less each week, which is about 1.67 hours less per week. If they're spending 2 hours less per day, that adds up to more than 100 minutes less per week. So this seems to align well. If they're spending less time on all household responsibilities, including dinner, then they might have less time to prepare dinner, but their dinners are still as good because they might be cooking more efficiently or using other methods. This directly addresses the discrepancy because it explains why they spend less time preparing but still have dinners of similar quality.\n\nWait, but let me think again. The context says that non-working adults spend more time preparing dinner each week, but their dinners are just as good. So if working adults are spending less time on all household tasks, including dinner, they might be cooking faster or more quickly, but still producing dinners that are just as good. So this would make sense because the time saved on other tasks could mean they cook quicker, but the quality remains the same.\n\nSo, putting it all together, Option D seems to be the best answer because it directly relates the time spent on dinner preparation to the time spent on other household tasks, which explains why they spend less time on dinner but still have dinners that are just as good in quality.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D\n\nThe reasoning is that Option D explains why working adults spend less time preparing dinner (due to spending less time on all household responsibilities) while their dinners are still of similar quality, thus resolving the discrepancy.",
