I'm not sure how that ties into the argument about rejecting 1%. Unless it's about whether the cheese is produced domestically, but the argument is more about the cost-benefit of rejection.\n\nD) Does rejecting cheese deter exporters from shipping substandard cheese? This seems directly relevant because if rejection leads to deterrence, then the 1% rejection rate might actually be effective in reducing substandard imports. If it's deterrence, then the benefits (reduced substandard imports) are real, which would make the argument about costs vs. benefits more valid. If not, maybe the benefits are minimal.\n\nSo, evaluating the argument, the key is whether the 1% rejection actually has real consequences. Option D addresses whether rejection deters exporters, which would mean that the 1% rejection is effective in reducing substandard cheese. If it does deter, then the benefits are real, and the argument about costs vs. benefits is more valid. If not, maybe the benefits are negligible.\n\nOption B is about cost reduction, which is part of the argument, but the argument is about whether the benefits (rejecting 1%) outweigh the costs. So knowing if costs have gone down would help, but the argument is more about the benefits. Option D is more about the effectiveness of the rejection process in reducing substandard imports, which directly ties into the benefits part of the argument.\n\nTherefore, the most useful option is D because it directly addresses whether the benefits of rejecting that 1% are real and significant.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D\n\nThe reasoning is that option D directly addresses whether the 1% rejection rate effectively reduces substandard imports, which is crucial for evaluating the argument about the benefits of rejecting that percentage.",
