      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out which option best supports the experts' opinions based on the given context. Let me start by understanding the context and the question.\n\nThe context talks about haze causing a lot of pollution, specifically mentioning that continuous exposure to high concentrations of pollutants can lead to 10% to 15% of people suffering from chronic eye inflammation and xerophthalmia. Experts believe that if air quality isn't improved soon, these diseases and complications will increase.\n\nThe question is asking which of the options (A, B, C, D) best supports these experts' opinions. So, I need to find the option that logically follows from the context and strengthens the argument that poor air quality leads to an increase in these eye diseases.\n\nLet me look at each option one by one.\n\nOption A: Toxic particles can irritate and damage people's eyes, and long-term contact will affect lacrimal gland cells. This seems relevant because it directly mentions the effect of toxic particles on the eyes, which ties into the context. It also talks about long-term contact affecting the lacrimal gland, which is related to eye health, specifically xerophthalmia. So this supports the idea that poor air quality causes eye issues.\n\nOption B: The improvement of air quality cannot be achieved in a short period of time, and many people have to work in a polluted environment. This is also relevant because it points out that if urgent measures aren't taken, air quality won't improve quickly. This ties into the experts' concern about the increase in diseases if air quality isn't addressed. However, it's more about the impracticality of immediate solutions rather than directly supporting the incidence of eye diseases.\n\nOption C: Cases of chronic eye inflammation and xerophthalmia are usually concentrated in the pollen season. This introduces another factor, pollen season, which might be related to other issues like allergies. While it's a possible cause, it doesn't directly address the link between air quality and eye diseases as clearly as option A does.\n\nOption D: 65% of the above surveyed patients with eye diseases are men between the ages of 20 and 40. This gives demographic information but doesn't directly support the experts' argument about air quality leading to more cases. It might be useful for understanding the affected population but isn't the strongest support for the main point.\n\nSo, comparing all options, option A directly addresses the effect of air pollution on eye health by mentioning toxic particles and their impact on the eyes, including the lacrimal gland. This makes it the best support for the experts' opinions.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A\n\nThe correct option is: A",
