s around it were formed from the same gas and dust at the same time. This seems like a key point. If both the moons and the planet were formed from the same material, that would mean that the composition of the planet would include whatever materials the moons have. Since the moons are made of water ice, the planet would likely have similar materials. This would support the idea that the planet's atmosphere has water because it was formed from the same source that includes water.\n\nSo, putting it all together, Option D directly links the composition of the moons to the planet's atmosphere because they share the same formation material. This makes the most sense as the best support for the statement.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D\n\nThe reasoning is that if the satellite and the planet were formed from the same gas and dust, the water present in the satellites would contribute to the planet's atmosphere, supporting the statement about Jupiter's water content.\n\nThe correct option is: D",
