      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to solve this logic problem about the company's general manager assistants and their departments. Let me try to break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, the context says there are six assistants: F, G, H, I, M, and P. They're managing three departments, and each department is under the charge of three assistants. So, each department has three assistants, and each assistant is in charge of at least one department. That means there are a total of 3 departments \u00d7 3 assistants each = 9 assistant-department assignments. Since there are six assistants, each assistant must be in charge of 1.5 departments on average, but since you can't have half a department, some are in charge of one, and some of two. But wait, the first condition says there's exactly one assistant in charge of three departments. So, one assistant is in charge of three departments, and the remaining five assistants are in charge of one or two departments each. Let me check: 3 departments \u00d7 3 assistants = 9 assignments. If one assistant has three, that's 3 assignments. So the remaining 6 assignments must be distributed among the other five assistants. That would mean two assistants have two departments each, and the rest have one each. Because 2\u00d72 + 3\u00d71 = 4 + 3 = 7, but we need 6. Hmm, maybe one assistant has two, and the others have one each. Let me recalculate: 3 (from the one assistant) + 2 (from another) + 4\u00d71 = 3+2+4=9. Yes, that works. So, one assistant has three departments, one has two, and four have one each.\n\nNow, the question is: If F and M are not in charge of the same department, which of the options must be correct?\n\nLet me list the options again:\n\nA) F and H are in the same department.\n\nB) F and I are in the same department.\n\nC) I and P are in the same department.\n\nD) M and G are in the same department.\n\nI need to figure out which of these must be true given the constraints.\n\nFirst, let's note the constraints:\