epartments. Wait, no, because each assistant can be in charge of multiple departments, but each department needs two more assistants. So, each of the five assistants must be in charge of exactly two departments because 5 assistants \u00d7 2 departments each = 10 assignments, but we only need 6. Hmm, that doesn't add up. Wait, perhaps some assistants are in charge of two departments, and others are in charge of one.\n\nWait, no, because F is already in charge of three departments, so each department needs two more assistants. So, total assistant-department assignments needed are 3\u00d72=6. So, the five assistants (G, H, I, M, P) must be assigned to these 6 slots. So, one of them must be in charge of two departments, and the others in charge of one each. Because 5 assistants can't cover 6 assignments unless one is in two departments.\n\nSo, let's say one assistant is in charge of two departments, and the others are in charge of one each. So, for example, G is in charge of two departments, and H, I, M, P are each in charge of one.\n\nBut wait, let's check the constraints:\n\n- Constraint 2: F and G cannot be in the same department. So, G cannot be in any department that F is in. But F is in all three departments, so G cannot be in any department. That's impossible because G has to be in charge of at least one department. So, this leads to a contradiction. Therefore, F cannot be the one in charge of three departments because that would force G to be in a department where F is, which is not allowed.\n\nSo, F cannot be the one managing all three departments. Therefore, the one managing all three departments must be someone else. Let's consider that someone else.\n\nLet's try M as the one managing all three departments. So, M is in charge of departments 1, 2, and 3. Then, F cannot be in the same department as M, so F must be in a different department. But since M is in all three, F must be in a department where M isn't? Wait, no, because M is in all three, so F can't be in any department where M is. But M is in all departments, so F can't be in any department, which is impossible because F must be in charge of at least one. Therefore, M cannot be the one managing all three departments either.\n\nWait, that can't be right. Let me think again. If M is the one managing all three departments, then each department has M plus two others. So, for each department, M is one, and two others. So, the other assistants (F, G, H, I, P) must be assigned to these departments, but F cannot be in the same department as M, which is all departments. So, F can't be in any department, which is impossible. Therefore, M cannot be the one managing all three departments.\n\nSo, the only remaining possibilities are H or I. Let's try H as the one managing all three departments.\n\nIf H is in charge of all three departments, then each department has H plus two others. So, the other assistants (F, G, I, M, P) must be assigned to these departments. Now, constraint 2: F and G cannot be in the same department. So, F and G must be in different departments.\n\nAlso, constraint 3: H and I cannot be in the same department. But H is in all three departments, so I cannot be in any department where H is, which is all departments. Therefore, I cannot be assigned to any department, which is impossible because I must be in charge of at least one. So, H cannot be the one managing all three departments either.\n\nWait, that's a problem. Let me check again. If H is the one managing all three departments, then each department has H plus two others. So, I cannot be in any department because H is in all, which violates constraint 3. Therefore, H cannot be the one managing all three departments.\n\nNow, let's try I as the one managing all three departments.\n\nIf I is in charge of all three departments, then each department has I plus two others. So, the other assistants (F, G, H, M, P) must be assigned to these departments. Now, constraint 3: H and I cannot be in the same department. But I is in all departments, so H cannot be in any department, which is impossible because H must be in charge of at least one. Therefore, I cannot be the one managing all three departments either.\n\nWait, this is a problem. All possibilities for the one managing all three departments lead to a contradiction. So, perhaps I made a mistake in my reasoning.\n\nWait, maybe I'm misunderstanding the constraints. Let me re-examine.\n\nThe first condition says there's exactly one general manager assistant in charge of three departments at the same time. So, only one assistant is in charge of three departments, and the others are in charge of one or two.\n\nBut perhaps the departments are separate, so each department is under the charge of three assistants, but the same assistant can be in charge of multiple departments.\n\nWait, but each department is under the charge of three assistants, so each department has three assistants assigned to it. So, for example, department 1 has assistants A, B, C; department 2 has D, E, F; etc. But the same assistant can be in charge of multiple departments.\n\nWait, but the problem says each general manager assistant is in charge of at least one department. So, each assistant is assigned to at least one department, but can be assigned to multiple.\n\nSo, the total number of assistant-department assignments is 3 departments \u00d7 3 assistants = 9. Since there are six assistants, each must be assigned to at least one, and one is assigned to three.\n\nSo, the one assigned to three departments is the one in charge of all three. The others are assigned to one or two departments.\n\nNow, let's try again.\n\nIf F is the one assigned to three departments, then F is in charge of all three. But constraint 2 says F and G cannot be in the same department. So, G cannot be in any department where F is. But F is in all three, so G cannot be in any department, which is impossible. So F cannot be the one assigned to three.\n\nSimilarly, if M is the one assigned to three, then M is in all three departments. So, each department has M plus two others. But constraint 2 says F and G cannot be in the same department. So, F and G must be in different departments. But since M is in all departments, F and G can't be in any department where M is, which is all departments. So F and G can't be assigned anywhere, which is impossible. So M cannot be the one assigned to three.\n\nIf H is the one assigned to three, then each department has H plus two others. Constraint 3 says H and I cannot be in the same department. But H is in all departments, so I can't be in any department, which is impossible. So H cannot be the one assigned to three.\n\nIf I is the one assigned to three, then each department has I plus two others. Constraint 3 says H and I cannot be in the same department. But I is in all departments, so H can't be in any department, which is impossible. So I cannot be the one assigned to three.\n\nWait, that leaves only G as the possible one assigned to three. Let's try that.\n\nIf G is assigned to three departments, then each department has G plus two others. Now, constraint 2 says F and G cannot be in the same department. But G is in all departments, so F cannot be in any department where G is, which is all departments. So F can't be in any department, which is impossible because F must be in charge of at least one. Therefore, G cannot be the one assigned to three.\n\nWait, this is a problem. All six assistants, when assigned to three departments, lead to a contradiction. So, perhaps I'm misunderstanding the problem.\n\nWait, maybe the departments are separate, and each department has three assistants, but the same assistant can be in multiple departments. So, for example, department 1 has assistants A, B, C; department 2 has D, E, F; etc. But the same assistant can be in multiple departments.\n\nWait, but the problem says each general manager assistant is in charge of at least one department. So, each assistant is ass