all departments. So F can't be assigned anywhere, which is impossible. So G cannot be the one assigned to three.\n\nWait, that leaves only F, M, H, I, G as possibilities, but all lead to contradictions. That can't be right. There must be a mistake in my reasoning.\n\nWait, perhaps the departments are separate, and each department has three assistants, but the same assistant can be in multiple departments. So, for example, department 1 has F, G, H; department 2 has F, I, M; etc. But each assistant must be in at least one department.\n\nWait, but the problem says each general manager assistant is in charge of at least one department. So, each assistant is assigned to at least one department, but can be assigned to multiple.\n\nSo, the total number of assistant-department assignments is 3 departments \u00d7 3 assistants = 9. Since there are six assistants, each must be assigned to at least one, and one is assigned to three.\n\nSo, the one assigned to three is in charge of all three departments. The others are assigned to one or two.\n\nNow, let's try again with this understanding.\n\nIf F is the one assigned to three departments, then F is in charge of all three. But constraint 2 says F and G cannot be in the same department. So, G cannot be in any department where F is, which is all departments. So G can't be assigned anywhere, which is impossible. So F cannot be the one assigned to three.\n\nSimilarly, if M is the one assigned to three, then M is in all three departments. So each department has M plus two others. Constraint 2 says F and G cannot be in the same department. So F and G must be in different departments. But since M is in all departments, F and G can't be in any department where M is, which is all departments. So F and G can't be assigned anywhere, which is impossible. So M cannot be the one assigned to three.\n\nIf H is the one assigned to three, then each department has H plus two others. Constraint 3 says H and I cannot be in the same department. So I 