      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which option weakens the argument presented in the context. The context talks about Bacillus thuringiensis (B. t.) toxins being preferable to chemical insecticides because they are specific and don't harm mammals or birds. Also, their limited range means they kill one species without affecting others that might prey on it.\n\nThe question is asking which statement, if true, would most weaken the argument. The argument is in favor of using B. t. toxins because they're better than chemical insecticides. So, I need to find an option that shows a weakness in this argument.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Chemical insecticides cause harm to a greater number of insect species than do B. t. toxins.\n- This suggests that while B. t. toxins are specific, they might not cover as many species as chemicals. But the argument is about specificity and not the number of species. The argument says B. t. toxins are better because they don't harm mammals or birds, not necessarily because they affect more species. So maybe this weakens the argument because if chemicals harm more species, but B. t. toxins are better for their specificity, it's a trade-off. But I'm not sure if this is the strongest point.\n\nB) No particular B. t. toxin is effective against all insects.\n- This points out that B. t. toxins aren't universally effective against all insects. So, if a particular toxin doesn't work on some insects, it might mean that you'd need multiple toxins, which could be less efficient or more complicated. This directly challenges the argument's point about their specificity because if they aren't effective against all insects, their effectiveness is limited, which is what the argument is highlighting as an advantage. So this seems like a strong candidate.\n\nC) B. t. toxins do not harm weeds that do damage to farm crops.\n- The context mentions that B. t. toxins don't harm mammals or birds, but this option talks about weeds. The argument doesn't address weeds, so this doesn't directly weaken the argument about B. t. toxins being better than chemicals because it's about a different issue. So probably not the answer.\n\nD) Insects build up resistance more readily to B. t. toxins than to chemical insecticides.\n- This suggests that B. t. toxins might lose effectiveness over time because insects develop resistance faster. If that's true, then B. t. toxins wouldn't be as effective in the long run, which would weaken their advantage. So this is another strong candidate.\n\nNow, comparing B and D. The argument is about the specificity and the limited range of B. t. toxins. Option B directly addresses the limitation of B. t. toxins not being effective against all insects, which weakens the argument's point about their effectiveness. Option D talks about effectiveness over time due to resistance, which also weakens their advantage.\n\nI think both B and D are good, but the question is which one most weakens the argument. The argument's main point is that B. t. toxins are better because they are specific and don't harm other species. If B. t. toxins aren't effective against all insects, that's a direct weakness in their effectiveness, which the argument relies on. So B seems more directly relevant.\n\nAlternatively, D is about the long-term effectiveness, which is also a weakness but perhaps not as directly tied to the argument's main point about specificity and limited range. So I think B is the better answer.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B",
      "logprobs": null,
