gument, not weakening it.\n\nD) Loux's grandson had repeatedly expressed his desire to own a farm. This is interesting. The grandson wanted the farm, but Loux's executor, Zembaty, had to choose which properties to sell. If the grandson wanted the farm, maybe Zembaty would have to sell it to him, even if Loux didn't object. But does this weaken the argument? The solicitor's point is that since Loux didn't object, she wouldn't have objected to selling it to her grandson. But if the grandson wanted it, maybe Zembaty had no choice but to sell it to him, making Loux's argument that she didn't object irrelevant. So, this could weaken the argument because it shows that selling to the grandson was necessary, perhaps because the grandson wanted it.\n\nWait, but the question is which option weakens the solicitor's argument. The solicitor's argument is that Loux didn't object, so she wouldn't have objected to selling it to her grandson. If the grandson wanted it, then Zembaty had to sell it to him, making Loux's argument that she didn't object irrelevant because she might have wanted it sold regardless. So, D weakens the argument.\n\nAlternatively, A and B both say selling the farm is necessary, which would mean Zembaty had no choice, so Loux didn't have an objection because she didn't care. So, A and B support the argument, not weaken it.\n\nC says Loux knew the executor had to satisfy legal requirements, which might mean she knew selling the farm was necessary, so again, supports the argument.\n\nSo, the correct answer is D because it shows that selling to the grandson was necessary, perhaps because the grandson wanted it, making Loux's argument that she didn't object irrelevant.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D",
