      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read through the context and the question again to make sure I understand everything.\n\nThe context says that for several centuries, there have been hairless dogs in western Mexico and coastal Peru. The argument then states that since hairlessness is a rare trait, it's unlikely that it developed twice independently. Because these dogs have never been in the wild before, and the vast mountainous jungle between the two regions made overland travel really hard back then, the argument concludes that the dogs must have been transported by boat from one region to the other during trading expeditions.\n\nNow, the question is asking which assumption the argument requires. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nI need to break down the argument to identify the missing piece, which is the assumption. The argument is using the fact that the trait is rare and that the regions are separated by a difficult terrain to conclude that the dogs were transported by boat. So, the argument is assuming that the only way the hairless dogs could have spread between these regions is through boat travel during trading.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Hairless dogs have never been found elsewhere. But the argument doesn't need this assumption because it's only about the regions mentioned. The argument doesn't care about other places, just that the two regions are the only ones with hairless dogs.\n\nB) It was easier to travel by boat between these regions than by land. This seems relevant because the argument uses the difficulty of overland travel to conclude that boat was used. So, if overland was actually easier, the argument's conclusion wouldn't hold. So this is a possible assumption.\n\nC) Hairless dogs were traded in exchange for other goods. The argument doesn't specify how the dogs were traded, just that they were transported by boat. So this isn't necessarily an assumption; the argument doesn't rely on this detail.\n\nD) Most trade goods in Mexico were transported by boat. Again, the argument doesn't focus on trade goods in general, just the transportation of dogs. So this isn't directly relevant.\n\nSo, the key assumption is that boat travel was the only feasible way, which is option B.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\nThe argument assumes that traveling by boat was easier between the two regions than by overland, which supports the conclusion that the dogs were transported by boat. \n\nAnswer: B",
