      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out which of the options A, B, C, or D is not an intellectual property based on the given definition. Let me start by understanding the context provided.\n\nThe context says that intellectual property is the right of people to enjoy the wealth created by intellectual labor in fields like science, technology, culture, education, and art. So, intellectual property is about protecting the fruits of human creativity and effort in these areas.\n\nNow, looking at the question: it asks which of the options is NOT an intellectual property. The options are Trademark right, Right of invention, Portrait rights, and Copyright.\n\nLet me go through each option one by one.\n\nOption A: Trademark right. I know that trademarks are used to protect brands, logos, and other distinctive signs that identify goods or services. They are a form of intellectual property because they protect creative designs and logos, which are intangible assets. So, A is an intellectual property.\n\nOption B: Right of invention. This refers to the legal protection granted for new and useful\u53d1\u660es. In many countries, inventors are granted patents, which are a type of intellectual property. So, B is also an intellectual property.\n\nOption C: Portrait rights. This is a bit trickier. Portrait rights typically refer to the legal rights a person has to control the use of their own photograph or image. This is more about personal rights and privacy rather than intellectual creation. It's not about creating something new but about the rights to one's own image. So, portrait rights might not fall under intellectual property.\n\nOption D: Copyright. Copyrights protect original works of authorship, like books, music, paintings, and software. They are definitely a form of intellectual property because they protect creative works. So, D is an intellectual property.\n\nPutting it all together, the odd one out is C) Portrait rights because they are more about personal rights and privacy rather than protecting intellectual creation.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C",
