 are actually by Clovio, but it doesn't mention anything about forgeries. It just talks about misattributions. So, C might not be necessarily true because the context doesn't discuss forgeries specifically, only misattributions.\n\nOption D: Contours and hatching are the main features that distinguish the drawing styles of different artists.\nThe context mentions that analyzing contour and hatching helps distinguish, but it doesn't say that they are the main features. It could be that other features are more important, but the context doesn't specify. So, D might not be necessarily true.\n\nWait, but let me think again. The context does say that the analysis of these stylistic features (contour and hatching) can help distinguish. So, it's possible that these are the main features used. But the context doesn't explicitly state that they are the main features, just that they are features used for analysis. So, D might not be necessarily true.\n\nWait, but the question is asking which must be true. So, looking back, the context says that the analysis can help distinguish, and the example shows that some works are misattributed. So, the fact that the analysis can help distinguish implies that there is a difference in the styles. So, maybe B is true because the analysis shows a difference between Clovio and Michelangelo.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the analysis can distinguish between any artists, not necessarily Clovio and Michelangelo. So, perhaps B is not necessarily true because the context doesn't specify that the difference is between Clovio and Michelangelo, just that the analysis can distinguish.\n\nWait, but the question is about which must be true. So, the context says that the analysis can help distinguish, and the example shows that some works are misattributed, implying that the analysis found a difference. So, perhaps B is true because the analysis found a difference between Clovio and Michelangelo.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the analysis can distinguish between a