      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read it again carefully.\n\nThe context says that in our solar system, only Earth fits to sustain life. But because of the large number of planetary systems in the universe, the argument concludes that many planets can sustain life. The question is asking what assumption the argument is making without justification, and the options are A to D.\n\nHmm, so the argument is using the fact that only Earth is suitable for life in our solar system, but since there are so many planetary systems, it infers that many can sustain life. But wait, the problem is that the argument assumes something about other planetary systems.\n\nLet me break it down. The argument is saying: If only Earth (out of many planets in our solar system) can sustain life, and there are many planetary systems in the universe, then the number of life-sustaining planets must be large. But this reasoning relies on an assumption that isn't stated.\n\nWhat's the missing link here? Well, the argument assumes that the number of planets in other systems is similar to Earth's. So, it's like saying, if in our solar system, only one out of nine can sustain life, and there are N planetary systems, then the total number is N*(1/9). But this only works if each planetary system has a similar ratio of Earth-like planets to others.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Life similar to Earth's could evolve under different conditions. This seems related to the idea that maybe other planets could have life without Earth's conditions. But the argument doesn't rely on this; it's more about the number of planets, not the possibility of life elsewhere.\n\nB) If a planet is Earth-like, life will arise on it. This is about the existence of life given Earth-like conditions. The argument doesn't depend on this assumption because it's about the number of Earth-like planets, not whether they will have life.\n\nC) Our solar system is similar to many others in the universe. This seems relevant because if our solar system is typical, then the ratio applies elsewhere. The argument presumes that other systems are like ours, so this is the missing assumption.\n\nD) Most other planetary systems have nine planets. This doesn't make sense because the argument is about Earth-like planets, not the total number of planets. So this option is probably incorrect.\n\nSo, the argument assumes that our solar system is a typical example, which is option C. Without this assumption, the argument wouldn't hold because it wouldn't know if other systems have similar ratios of Earth-like planets.\n</think>\n\nThe argument assumes that our solar system is similar to others in the universe, allowing the ratio of Earth-like planets to be applied elsewhere. This is option C.\n\nThe correct option is: C",
