      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which of the options A, B, C, or D is a negative image based on the given definitions. Let me start by understanding what continuous contrast, positive images, and negative images mean from the context provided.\n\nThe context says that continuous contrast is when there's a visual dislocation in color contrast over time. Positive images are when the original object's color is retained temporarily after the visual stimulation stops. Negative images are when the object is colored with the original color after the stimulation stops.\n\nSo, the question is asking which option describes a negative image. That means I'm looking for a situation where, after the object is no longer seen, the memory of its color still appears, causing a contrast.\n\nLet me go through each option one by one.\n\nOption A: A continuous projection of a static screen at 24 frames per second creates a dynamic picture. Hmm, this sounds like it's talking about the visual effect of the screen itself, not about the retention of color after the object is removed. It's more about how the screen's movement creates a dynamic image, so probably not a negative image.\n\nOption B: After staring at a red object, even if you move it away, you still feel red. Wait, that sounds like the color is still perceived even after the object is moved. But the definition says negative image is when the image is colored with the original object's color after the stimulation stops. So if you move the object away, the stimulation stops, and the color is still perceived as red. That fits the definition of a negative image because the color is still there even after the object is removed.\n\nOption C: After staring at red for a long time, vision moves to white quickly, not white but green. This seems like a different kind of visual phenomenon, maybe related to afterimages or color shifts. It doesn't directly relate to the retention of the original color after the object is removed, so I don't think this is a negative image.\n\nOption D: Red paired with yellow, sometimes seen as purple, sometimes as green. This is talking about color mixing or perception, not about the retention of color after the object is removed. So it's more about how colors interact rather than the negative image concept.\n\nPutting it all together, Option B is the one where the color is still perceived after the object is moved away, which aligns with the definition of a negative image.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\nThe reasoning is that Option B describes the retention of the original color (red) even after the object is moved away, fitting the definition of a negative image.\n\nThe correct option is: B",
